In order to progress, a few things need to be laid out.

What is sustainability?

It is something that is holistic, it is everything, and yet it is ethereal — there is no one object that in and of itself is "sustainable"

±±±

The concepts contained in this selection of essays are more useful as a whole rather than in in any singular detail. Each principle or chapter is useful on its own, but sustainability itself is most importantly a combination of all that is contained within.

±±±

The problem with sustainability is that it is so incredibly complex, yet it is so simple.

It is a way of thinking, a way of being, its philosophy, we must search for the correct argument.

±±±

My favorite two texts I've read on the subject of sustainability are Christopher Alexander's ''The Timeless Way of Building'', and the book ''Eternally Yours''. Neither is particularly new, and one of them, ''Eternally Yours'', was the outcome of an art/design collective … What intrigues me the most about them, what makes the ideas the most sustainable, is that they ar[e]

The text I've found most helpful thus far in my thinking about the subject of sustainability has been ''Eternally Yours''. What is most interesting is that it basically came out of an art/design weekend conference; however, it is the only piece I've read that has really challenged the way in which we think about a product. As far as I am concerned, it is the best way.

[[Cradle to Cradle]] just tries to force eco-safe or eco-enhancing materials into an otherwise unchanged capitalist system. Now McDonough and crew do it in about the best possible way they could, but it really isn't enough.

Also, don't get me wrong, I love making money, and I want everyone to make money, but that can't be the only goal.

±±±

What do you do as a sustainable designer when your goal is to make a product that is only repaired, upgraded, and enhanced, but not replaced. Let's leave the ephemeral behind — unless it is required, which sometimes it is conceptually or physically (read more about this in "durability") —

±±±

There are rules, and they vary in complexity, but it isn't impossible to reach "true sustainability"

