!! Working Definitions

What is sustainable graphic design? It seems simple to label things like architecture, economic development and farming with the adjective of sustainable, but how does it change graphic design?

One key aspect is in taking on a series of key principles to the operation of a design practice.

Sustainable graphic design has to be about more than simply using less-harmful inks and printing methods. It has to be more than requesting high recycled content paper. There is more than just switching office light bulbs and buying green-energy credits. But what is it?

For starters it is the mind set and philosophy set for the creation of things. Have you accepted efficiency, thrift, brevity and simplicity as your friends? Are you prepared to set up constraints for all of your work to fall under? What existing things are working for you? Leave those as they are. What can change next? Keep doing this until you can think of nothing else to change...

Sustainability is not about static-ness or design sameness, it is simply about a common set of tools and ideologies that can be applied to any and all fields or applications...

I finally figured it out… Graphic Design is talking about what to do in terms of paper and printing because, as per usual, it is late to the party. The concepts of mitigation and safely sourced supply is becoming much more commonplace to architecture and the design and production of products, so they are moving on to more complicated, higher level matters of philosophy and ideology. Graphic design just hasn't gotten this far yet, we are still trying to figure out where to get recycled paper and energy credits from.

Part of sustainable graphic design will be this area of limiting options to only environmentally, socially and economically sensible ones. Things like limiting printed in the office, no comps, etc… The other part will have to be more than that however. We need up to date ideologies, new mentalities, new approaches. The guts to decide whether or not something even needs to exist in the first place. Part of graphic design becoming sustainable will be in growing a pair and telling clients and the public that sometimes you don't need that new brand, or mailer, or periodical, whatever. We have the power and ability to do that as well…

Maybe sustainable graphic design is just a late bloomer?

It is not that the acts of mitigation, reduction, etc. are not important, it is just that they cannot make up a whole solution. The most important part of the solution will be new ideas, concepts, approaches, etc.

±±±

Sustainability in design, design in sustainability.

This investigation began with reading a fictional story, a cookbook, essays on eating well and maintaining your own farm, and finally, a couple of documentaries about climate change. With pieces ranging from ''Who Killed the Electric Car'' to ''Animal, Vegetable, Miracle'' as a foundation, my search into what sustainability was headed into the realm of design. Sustainability in Design, Design in Sustainability—what do the two have to give each other? Answering this question has been the driving force for much of what I have covered in subject and breadth.

Sustainability is not hard, nor is it easy. It is a state of mind. It takes into account everything that is around us. While seemingly ground breaking and innovative, it is a way of thought that has existed for a long time, and requires little that is new or "extra" in implementation. Considerations come from environmental concerns (does this require burning down forests?) to economics (will it sell?) to social concerns (does it provide a living wage?).

In reading, searching and thinking about how the arenas of sustainability and design interact I found commonalities in works coming at the topic from many different angles. I have tried to evolve these commonalities into principles that can help guide further investigation and process.

Much of what I uncovered has popped up time and time again in contemporary thought and literature. This prompted two trains of thought: do these issues just not really concern people? and am I just rehashing the past? The answer to both questions is no. People do generally seem to care, they just don't always know how. And while what I am attempting comes from all of these existing sources, I am approaching it in (what I think) is a novel way.

Silent Spring is the foundation for much of what we call "environmentalism." Rachel Carson was indebted to Thoreau however… Walden sets up much of our philosophy in regards to a sustainable society and approach to living…

[[Cradle to Cradle]] is the modern version of these works, it incorporates all the different realms and ideologies of these texts into a contemporary discussion on the design and manufacture of our products. This was where my quest for sustainability in design really took off.

C2C sets up the concept of "Use, Not Own" which is key in the understanding of my [[Service Principle|Services]]. Service is the idea that what we want are the services objects provide, and not usually the objects themselves. For example, people buy cellular phones for the goal of making calls—of communicating (communicating has become more than calls now I realize...). A phone need not also be an mp3 player, etc. it needs to make calls, and perhaps send/receive text and email, and in some cases provide internet access… However, we have bastardized the phone into a status symbol that must be upgraded at every possible chance. Likewise with cars, most clothing, etc. The idea that "fashion" may be something you want make sense, but why own what you know will be obsolete—what is intentionally made obsolete by the next rollout of product. With this in mind, durable goods should be designed durably to last longer than a single "style" cycle, perhaps by being less "styled" and more "designed." … This can be applied to many other things as well: cooling, heating, washing, food, transportation—anything where we are after a provision and not necessarily a physical, immutable thing. [[Milk bottles|A Quart of Milk]] are one example: we want milk, so the milk-man brings us bottles filled with milk, when we drink the milk, empty bottles are picked up and refilled with milk… easy for you, you don't even have to go to the store anymore!

Time passing and affecting a design is tied to ideas outlined by "service." Time must be taken account in a thing, because eventually something will have to be done with said thing. Washing machines wear out, cars rust, jeans can only be patched so many times, we get old and die. Time has its way with everyone. We can either reject this and create things that are meant to last forever, outlast us, outlast civilization, outlast environmental destruction (like most plastic) or we can take it into account. Time can be accepted in a number of ways. One way is in how long things last. Things should be made to last as long as they are intended for use. An important building (like renaissance cathedrals, roman temples, etc.) is understandably built for permanence. A carton of milk, or candy bar wrapper, not so much so. A second way to take time into account is to let time go by and let users and makers actually learn about their products. The best way to make a product better? Use it, find what is wrong, fix that, use it again, fix what is "bad" again, and repeat until you have perfected the thing, or have run out of resources... whichever happens first.

